</ul><h1>Cute & Useful</h1><p>During a lull in conversation, Annie released a satisfied sigh and took in the night. “It’s just nice to eat dinner outside,” she said. “Lizzie and Will were so happy, they even ate all their veggies!”</p>
<p>Eleven-year-old Lizzie smiled and rolled her eyes, but Will, age six, seemed genuinely confused. “Corn is a vegetable?” he said.</p>
<p>Everyone laughed heartily at that.</p>
<p>“Won’t be long before it’s <em>too</em> hot, if you can believe it,” said Uncle Jake, who was visiting the family from back East.</p>
<p>“Hey, that reminds me,” Anthony said, “Meh has these for sale today, take a look.”</p>
<p>“Aww,” Annie said, taking the phone and passing it to their children. “They’re little bears. And they’re fans?”</p>
<p>“Also, flashlights and power banks,” Anthony said. “We should get them.”</p>
<p>“Fun colors,” Annie said. “Brown bears are grizzlies, I know. And white bears are polar bears, obviously. But I’ve never heard of a navy blue bear before!”</p>
<p>This time, they all laughed but one, Uncle Jake.</p>
<p>“Probably just a black bear that’s been swimming,” Uncle Jake said.</p>
<p>“Sorry?” Annie said.</p>
<p>“A navy blue bear,” Uncle Jake clarified, “is probably just a black bear that’s gone swimming. The water would make its coat appear iridescent and therefore dark blue.”</p>
<p>“Jake,” Anthony said.</p>
<p>But Jake didn’t seem to hear him. “Or perhaps it’s a black bear cub, fresh from the womb, still wet with the fluids of birth.”</p>
<p>Anthony took a long drink of his wine while Annie looked down and muttered, under her breath, “Jesus.”</p>
<p>“I want the pink one!” Lizzie said, who was old enough to understand when a situation required diffusing.</p>
<p>“That one,” Jake said, “is probably just a polar bear who’s just enjoyed a fresh kill.”</p>
<p>“C’mon, bro,” Anthony hissed.</p>
<p>Again, Jake seemed not to hear him. “Maybe there’s been a bit of rain, so the starkness of the blood on its coat has faded considerably, making it appear pink rather than dark red.”</p>
<p>“What did it kill?” Will asked.</p>
<p>“Will,” Annie said.</p>
<p>“Probably a seal,” Jake said. “But maybe a walrus or a beluga. A hiker, possibly, if it was real hungry.”</p>
<p>A silence fell over the table.</p>
<p>Jake took it upon himself to break it. “Great dinner, Annie. Say, what’s on the agenda for tomorrow?”</p>
<p>“We planned to go to the zoo,” Annie said.</p>
<p>“No!” cried the children in unison, before they both slid out of their seats and ran crying inside.</p>
<p>“Huh,” Uncle Jake said, sitting back. “That’s funny. I used to love the zoo when I was their age.”</p>